## 24. Signed contributions with a rank-one upper-digit map

Fix the unit parameters $h,m,g$ and the balanced pivot basis of Theorem 21.2, with $p>3$. Use the centered short frequency box of scale $H\asymp p^{5/8-\delta/2}$ with $0<\delta\le1/100$ and separate translated pivot rectangles of side $A<p^2$. Put $U=1+A/p^{3/2}$. A graph has inventory $N(T)$ equal to its number of short points $v=Tu$ with the unit determinant condition. Consider graphs whose short points span a rational three-plane, with $N(T)\ge Y\gg H$.

For any fixed pair of lower labels, restrict the two exact modulus-$p^2$ base phases to the literal graph. Their product with the weight is a function of $u$ of absolute value at most one. The upper-digit increments use $v=Tu$ modulo $p$ and do not use its higher digit. In fact the increment already has a factor $p$ in the exponent modulo $p^2$. Thus varying carries stay in the base factor, independently of both upper pivot digits. The short projection to $u\bmod p$ is injective. Parseval in the auxiliary upper-digit difference $\gamma$, followed by Cauchy–Schwarz, bounds a single lower-pair contribution by $p^3\sqrt{N(T)\mathcal E}$, where $\mathcal E$ counts upper-digit pair collisions. For digit difference vectors $d,d'$ in an $O(U)$ box and lower phase matrices $M,M'$, write $K$ for the number of equalities $M^{\mathsf T}d=M'^{\mathsf T}d'$. Then $\mathcal E\ll U^4 K$.

Attach to each three-plane a primitive integral normal $(a_N,b_N)$ of height $k$. The product of the three successive minima of its short graph lattice is $O(H^3/N(T))$. A nonzero exterior product of three independent graph vectors has all coordinates divisible by $p$, because the graph modulo $p$ has rank two. Dividing by its content shows $k\ll H^3/(pY)$. The projected inputs span $\mathbb F_p^2$ since a one-dimensional input projection gives $O(H)$ points. Thus $b_N^{\mathsf T}T=-a_N^{\mathsf T}$, with $b_N$ nonzero in a finite graph chart. The saturated normal lattice has determinant comparable to $k$. Imposing the graph has index $p$ in it, so each rank-three graph on this normal has $N(T)\ll H^3/(pk)$ by successive minima. Also $\sum_T N(T)\ll pH^3/k$ for the normal family. At each actual frequency pair the simultaneous conditions $Tu=v$ and $b_N^{\mathsf T}T=-a_N^{\mathsf T}$ determine an affine line of matrices. There are $O(H^3/k)$ literal pairs in the normal plane, as is seen by solving for a coordinate of the normal of size $k$ and counting the compatible residue triples modulo $k$.

